J1188,Q26,MSMED Act delayed payment Supreme Court judgments,R-SANSAD-064,Rajya Sabha Unstarred Q. 900 (27 Jul 2026): Delayed Payments to MSMEs,UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 900,"UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 900TO BE ANSWERED ON 27.07.2026
DELAYED PAYMENTS TO MSMEs900.  SHRI CHANDRAKANT DAMODAR HANDORE:
Will the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:
(a)  whether Government is aware of the increasing incidence of delayed payments to MSMEs
by the Central Ministries, Public Sector Undertakings, Government agencies and large private
companies;
(b) the total amount of outstanding dues payable to MSMEs during each of the last three years;
(c)  whether Government has assessed the impact of such delays on employment, production
and business closures; and
(d)  the steps taken to ensure timely payments  under the provisions of the Micro, Small and
Medium Enterprises Development (MSMED) Act, including penalties for defaulting entities?
(a) to (d):  As per Section 15 of the Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises Development
(MSMED) Act, 2006 payment to the MSEs is to be made within 45 days. Under the provisions of
this Act, MSEFCs have been set up in the States/UTs to deal with cases of delayed payments of the
MSEs.
As reported by Central Ministries, Central Public Sector Enterprises (CPSEs)  and
Government Agencies on SAMADHAAN Portal, the cumulative dues towards MSMEs till June,
2026 were Rs. 48,209.46 crore, of which Rs. 31,452.73 crore has been reported as paid . Dues
amounting to Rs. 16,032 crore are within 45 days and Rs 724 crore are pending for over 45 days.
Government has taken a number of steps to ensure timely payments to MSEs, including:
i. Ministry of MSME launched SAMADH AAN Portal on 30.10.2017
(http://samadhaan.msme.gov.in/MyMSME/MSEFC/ MSEFCWelcomer.aspx .) for filing of
grievances and monitoring of outstanding dues to the Micro and Small Enterprises from the
buyers of goods and services. The Ministry of MSME created a s pecial sub -portal within
SAMADHAAN Portal on 14.06.2020, after the Aatma Nirbhar Bharat announcements, for
reporting the dues and monthly payments by Central Ministries/Department/Public Sector
Enterprises to MSMEs.
ii. Government launched Online Dispute Resolution (ODR) portal on 27.06.2025 to strengthen
the existing SAMADHAAN Portal, with the objective of ensuring speedy, cost -effective,
and transparent resolution of delayed payment disputes involving MSE. All fresh cas es
related to delayed payments are filed by MSEs on the MSME ODR portal with effect from
15.10.2025. As on 14.07.2026, 26,997 cases have been registered on the ODR Portal.
iii. Introduction of a pre -litigation, dialogue -based mechanism under the ODR framework
encourages voluntary mediation, conciliation or negotiation between the buyer and seller
before the matter proceeds to formal adjudication by MSEFCs.
iv. Government vide notifi cation S.O. 4845(E) dated 07.11.2024 has directed CPSEs and all
companies with the turnover of Rs. 250 Crore or more to on -board the Trade Receivables
Discounting System (TReDS), an electronic platform for facilitating the discounting of trade
receivables of MSMEs through multiple financiers.
v. Government vide notification S.O. 3507(E) dated 30.06.2026 has mandated all the CPSEs to
onboard on any one of the Trade Receivables Discounting System (TReDS) platform.",,,,,,
